          "answer": "Based on reading `src/shipping/carriers.py`, here's what it does:\n\n## Carrier Selection Module\n\n**Three shipping carriers with different rate structures:**\n\n| Carrier | Base Rate | Per-kg Rate |\n|---------|-----------|-------------|\n| ravenpost | $4.20 | $0.85/kg |\n| tortoise | $2.10 | $1.60/kg |\n| kestrel | $6.00 | $0.40/kg |\n\n**Key functions:**\n\n1. **`quote(name, weight_kg)`** (line 12-15)\n   - Calculates shipping cost: `round(base + per_kg * weight_kg, 2)`\n   - Returns the total cost rounded to 2 decimal places\n\n2. **`cheapest(weight_kg)`** (line 18-21)\n   - Filters carriers that haven't failed 3+ times (`_failures[n] < 3`)\n   - Returns the carrier with the lowest quote for the given weight\n   - This is the main selection function\n\n3. **`record_failure(name)`** (line 24-27)\n   - Increments the failure count for a carrier\n   - Returns the updated failure count\n   - When a carrier hits 3 failures, it's excluded from future `cheapest()` selections\n\n## How it connects to the runbook:\nAs noted in `docs/runbook.md`, this module \"keeps a per-carrier failure count. Three failures in a row and the carrier is skipped for the rest of the run; the next cheapest one takes over. This is silent by design \u2014 check the counts before assuming a bug.\"",
